## 3. User Experience

### 3.1 Key Flows
1. **First launch → Spatial Home.** Landscape immersive stereo. The Spatial Dock (Home, Apps, Browser, Media, Gallery, Settings, Environments, Control Center) floats below the workspace. A **gaze reticle** fills a radial ring over interactive targets (default 1.0 s dwell, configurable 0.5–2.0 s). Instant select: gamepad A/Enter, or a tap on the screen/headset button where available.
   - **Out-of-headset path**: the first screen shows a brief "Put your phone in the headset" card with an "I'm using it in 2D / set up first" option leading to the Compose hub.
2. **Window multitasking.** Windows (App Library, Browser, Media3 Player, Gallery, glTF Viewer, Optics/Quick Settings, Diagnostics) sit on a spherical arc, world-locked. Grab the title bar to change azimuth/elevation; push/pull depth (1.2–4.5 m); corner handles resize; minimize to a dock pill; close.
   - **Limit**: max 4 active content windows (live WebView/video count against it); extra windows auto-suspend to a snapshot.
3. **Passthrough (experimental).** Safety modal ("Stationary use only; never walk, cycle or drive") → `CAMERA` permission → CameraX frame on a full-bleed background quad. Denial falls back to virtual environments, never crashes.
4. **Mode A ↔ Mode B switch** (CameraX ↔ ARCore) uses an explicit release-then-acquire state machine.
5. **2D Hub (Compose).** Tabs: **Display, Tracking, Camera, Optics, Performance**, with live dual-viewport preview.
6. **Launching a third-party app.** A stereo notice explains that the stereo view will be left. Optional: post a notification "Return to PALINDORME XR" so the user can come back without removing the phone.
7. **Hand control (opt-in, experimental).** Settings → Input → "Hand control" starts a 20-second guided setup: safety/privacy note, camera permission, a "show your hand" check with live skeleton overlay, a pinch calibration (measures your pinch open/closed range), and a short practice target. Afterward: **look at a target (head-gaze reticle) and pinch thumb-to-index to select**, pinch and move to drag or scroll, pinch with both hands to resize, and raise an open palm toward the headset to open the Control Center. If hands are lost for more than ~1.5 s, a subtle indicator appears and gaze dwell / gamepad keep working with no interruption.

### 3.2 Comfort & Motion-Sickness Design
- **No artificial locomotion or camera motion**; the only moving element is the user's head.
- **Horizon-stable, low-latency rendering is a comfort feature**: if frame time exceeds budget for >2 s, drop render scale before dropping frame rate.
- **Recommended session break prompt** after 30 min (dismissible, configurable).
- **Reticle and windows never lock to the head** (no head-locked content except the reticle and the brief HUD).
- **First-use guidance**: sit down, 10 min first session.
- **Pre-filled warnings**: not for children under the headset manufacturer's recommended age; stop if dizzy.

### 3.3 Visual Identity — "Atmospheric Spatial Obsidian"
- **Palette**: Canvas `#060913` · Glass `#121A2B` @ 78 % with 1 dp `#38BDF8` @ 28 % edge · Primary `#38BDF8` · Secondary `#818CF8` · Warning `#F59E0B` · Error `#FB7185`.
- **Contrast rule**: all text on glass ≥ 4.5:1 (7:1 for text smaller than 18 sp inside 3D windows, since distortion and low-PPI optics blur edges). Verify during Stage 1 with an automated contrast check.
- **Typography (bundled)**: Space Grotesk (headers) · Plus Jakarta Sans (body) · JetBrains Mono (telemetry). **Minimum text size in-headset: 20 sp-equivalent at 2 m** because phone panels' pixel density limits legibility through the lenses.
- **Six GPU-light environments**: Minimal Futuristic Lab · Space Station · Modern Digital Workspace · Dark Futuristic Room · Nature (dusk, low-poly mountains, fireflies) · Abstract Floating Grid. Each exposes tint, ambient light, particle density, effects toggle and brightness. All are procedural (no large textures), under 1.5 MB total GPU memory per environment.
- **OLED note**: the Dark Room and Space Station use true black to save power and increase perceived contrast.

