## 5. Technical Architecture

### 5.1 Component Diagram
```text
┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                  MainActivity (Immersive, Edge-to-Edge, Landscape)         │
│  ┌───────────────────────────────┐    ┌─────────────────────────────────┐  │
│  │   Stereo GL Surface           │    │ Compose Settings & Calibration  │  │
│  │ (OpenGL ES 3.0 dual viewport) │◄──►│ (Display/Track/Cam/Optics/Perf) │  │
│  └──────────────┬────────────────┘    └────────────────┬────────────────┘  │
└─────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────┼───────────────────┘
                  │ lock-free snapshots / StateFlow      │
                  ▼                                      ▼
┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                          XRLauncher Coordinator                            │
│ HeadTrackingEngine · InputInteractionManager · XRSettingsManager           │
│ SpatialWindowManager · VirtualContentHost · AndroidAppManager              │
│ StereoRenderingEngine · VirtualEnvironmentManager · CameraModeCoordinator  │
│ CameraEngine (CameraX) · ARPerceptionEngine (ARCore) · PerformanceMonitor  │
│ HandTrackingEngine · GestureRecognizer · HandRayInteractor                 │
│ SharedCameraFrameSource (one camera owner, many consumers)                 │
│ SpatialKeyboard · ComfortManager · VoiceCommandManager (opt-in)            │
└────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

### 5.2 Threading Rules
- **GL thread**: only reads immutable snapshots; no allocations, no I/O, no locks in `onDrawFrame`. Pre-allocated matrices/vectors; object pools for events.
- **Sensor thread**: dedicated `HandlerThread`; writes the latest orientation into a seqlock or triple-buffer.
- **Content thread**: `VirtualContentHost` draws views at ≤ 30 fps for static UI, ≤ native rate for video/web scrolling.
- **IO/Default**: app discovery, DataStore, telemetry; expose `StateFlow`.
- **Interaction loop** (per frame): raycast gaze or controller ray against window planes and dock items → dwell timers → dispatch click / drag / push-pull / resize.

### 5.3 Rendering Pipeline
1. Update head pose (predicted) → per-eye view matrices with IPD offset.
2. Render environment, windows (glass shader), reticle, glTF to **left/right eye FBOs** (render-scale adjustable 0.6–1.0).
3. **Distortion pass**: use a **pre-computed distortion mesh** (e.g., 40×40 per eye) with per-channel UVs for chromatic aberration rather than a full-screen fragment solve. This is cheaper on mobile GPUs.
4. Present side by side to the phone display, respecting the display's refresh rate. Request the highest stable mode via `preferredDisplayModeId`, keep screen on, and set the window to `FLAG_KEEP_SCREEN_ON` and sustained-performance mode where supported.
5. Calibration: a two-eye grid screen lets the user adjust IPD, per-eye vertical offset, distortion strength and FOV; results persist per device.

### 5.4 Performance Budgets (targets, verified on the device matrix)
| Metric | Target |
| :--- | :--- |
| Frame time | ≤ 11.1 ms at 90 Hz, ≤ 16.6 ms at 60 Hz, p95 under budget |
| Motion-to-photon (est.) | ≤ 30 ms with prediction |
| Memory | < 600 MB total with 3 windows; GL textures < 200 MB |
| Cold start to first stereo frame | < 2.5 s |
| Battery | ≤ ~25 %/hour on a reference mid-range device with a static scene (measured, not promised) |

**Adaptive quality tiers** (driven by `PowerManager.getCurrentThermalStatus()` on API 29+, plus frame-time history and battery): render-scale reduction → effects off → environment particles off → 60 Hz lock → thermal warning banner → suspend passthrough/ARCore. Use ADPF `PerformanceHintManager` on API 31+ for the GL and content threads. Low-Power Mode toggles a conservative tier.

### 5.5 Persistence & Settings
Jetpack DataStore (Proto or Preferences) with schema versioning and migrations. Settings: optics profile per device, dwell time, comfort options, environment parameters, favorites/recents, voice opt-in. Corrupt-store recovery resets to defaults without crashing.

### 5.6 Privacy & Security
- Camera frames stay in memory, are never written to disk or sent over the network.
- No network permission beyond `INTERNET` for the Browser window; no analytics or ads SDKs; crash logs stay local unless the user shares them.
- Browser: no autofill of saved credentials, HTTPS-only upgrade where possible, per-session cookie option, a visible URL bar in-window, safe-browsing enabled.
- Voice: on-device recognition preferred (`createOnDeviceSpeechRecognizer`, API 33+); on older versions the app states clearly that audio may be processed by the system speech service.
- Manifest: `android:required="false"` for ARCore, camera, gyroscope-dependent features; `uses-feature` flags so Play does not filter out compatible phones.

### 5.7 Accessibility
- Every dock item and window control has a semantic label (for TalkBack when used in 2D).
- Adjustable dwell time, optional audio click, "large text" window scale, high-contrast glass option, reduce-motion setting (disables particles and parallax).
- Left-handed controller mapping.
- All settings are reachable without gaze (Compose hub with normal touch).
- Hand control is never required: every action has a gaze-dwell or gamepad equivalent. A one-handed mode (right or left) and a "relaxed pinch" sensitivity setting support limited dexterity.

### 5.8 Hand Interaction System

**Goal**: an Apple Vision Pro / Galaxy XR-style interaction model — *look to target, pinch to select* — built honestly for a phone in a passive headset. Phones have **no eye tracking**, so "look" is **head-gaze** (the reticle at the center of view). Hands come from the **rear camera** only.

#### 5.8.1 Pipeline
1. **Frame source**: `SharedCameraFrameSource` is the single owner of the rear camera and feeds consumers: the passthrough OES quad, the hand tracker, and ARCore (via its CPU image when in ARCore mode). This keeps `CameraModeCoordinator` rules intact (Section 4.2) because hand tracking is a *consumer*, not a second camera owner.
2. **Inference**: `HandTrackingEngine` runs an on-device hand landmark model (default: MediaPipe Tasks Vision *Hand Landmarker*, 21 landmarks per hand, up to 2 hands, GPU delegate with CPU fallback). It runs on its own `HandlerThread` at a target of ~30 fps using CameraX `ImageAnalysis` (`STRATEGY_KEEP_ONLY_LATEST`) at a low resolution (e.g., 640×480), so it never blocks the GL thread.
3. **Filtering**: landmarks pass through a per-landmark One-Euro filter (stronger smoothing at rest, light when moving) plus a short prediction step to hide inference latency.
4. **Pose to 3D**: the camera is mounted on the phone, so hands are expressed in camera space and mapped into head-relative world space using the current head pose. Depth uses the model's world-scale landmarks, with the user's calibrated palm width as the scale reference. Hands are drawn as a minimal translucent skeleton or soft fingertip dots (a toggleable overlay), never as a photo.
5. **Gesture recognition** (`GestureRecognizer`, pure Kotlin, unit-tested): a small state machine per hand with hysteresis. Output events are consumed by `InputInteractionManager` exactly like gamepad input.
6. **Interaction**: `HandRayInteractor` supplies target selection and manipulation (below).

#### 5.8.2 Gesture Vocabulary

| Gesture | Detection | Action |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Look + Pinch** (primary) | Thumb-tip to index-tip distance, normalized by palm width, crosses a close threshold (e.g., < 0.25) then releases above an open threshold (> 0.45; hysteresis prevents flicker) | Select / click the target under the gaze reticle. Fires on **release** after a pinch of < 400 ms, like a tap. |
| **Pinch-hold + move** | Pinch stays closed beyond the tap time | Drag a window (azimuth/elevation), drag a slider, or scroll content. Hand movement maps to angular motion with adjustable gain. The *target is locked* at pinch start so head motion doesn't change what you're dragging. |
| **Pinch-hold + push/pull** | Closed pinch, hand moves toward/away from the camera (hand scale change) | Window depth (1.2–4.5 m), clamped and eased. |
| **Two-hand pinch** | Both hands pinch the same window | Resize (spread/close) and rotate in yaw; resizing is clamped to min/max sizes. |
| **Direct poke (near targets)** | Index fingertip within ~3 cm of a window's virtual surface, within reach | Direct touch: tap or scroll without gaze. Works well for the Spatial Keyboard and sliders. Shows a small fingertip hover glow. |
| **Palm open toward headset** | Open palm, facing the camera, held ~0.6 s | Open or close the Control Center; shows a ring filling during the hold. |
| **Pinch on the dock pill / handle** | Same as Look+Pinch | Minimize, restore or close windows. |
| **Two-finger scroll** (optional) | Index + middle extended, move vertically | Scroll the focused window without needing a tap-hold. |
| **Cancel** | Hands drop out of frame or an open palm is shown during a drag | Drag is cancelled and the window returns to its prior position. |

Each gesture has a **debounce** (minimum 3 consistent frames), a **confidence gate** (tracking score threshold), and a **per-hand dominant** setting. Look+Pinch needs no raising of the arm: a relaxed hand in the lower-center field of view is enough.

#### 5.8.3 Ray, Targeting and Feedback
- **Primary targeting is head-gaze**; the pinch only confirms. This matches the "eyes + hand" mental model and is more accurate than pointing with a monocular hand pose.
- **Hand-ray mode** (optional, in settings): a ray from the shoulder-estimated origin through the index finger/wrist lets users point at targets instead of using head-gaze. It is more sensitive to tracking error, so it is off by default and uses a larger hit-target snap radius.
- **Target snapping and magnetism**: the reticle slightly snaps to interactive element centers within a small angular radius; larger hit-boxes for dock icons and handles (minimum ~48 dp-equivalent at 2 m).
- **Feedback** (hands have no physical click): hover highlight, reticle morphs into a small ring when a hand is detected, a ring-contract animation as the pinch closes, a soft audio tick on select, and **haptic pulse on the phone** where the device allows it. All can be toggled.
- **Tracking-lost UX**: a faint "hand not visible" glyph near the bottom of the view; gaze dwell is auto-enabled (configurable) while hands are lost so the user is never stuck.

#### 5.8.4 Setup & Personalization
- **Calibration**: measures palm width, pinch closed/open range, and the comfortable hand zone in the camera frame. Stored per user profile (DataStore).
- **Handedness**: left, right, or both. Left-handed mirror mapping is automatic when the model reports handedness (note: the camera image may be mirrored — a unit test pins down this convention).
- **Lighting check**: if the mean frame luminance is too low or the hand is repeatedly lost, a hint explains "Move to a brighter area".
- **Camera-visibility check**: if no hand is ever detected during setup and the frame looks dark/blocked, the app explains that the headset may block the camera and hand control isn't possible with this headset, then keeps gaze/gamepad.

#### 5.8.5 Performance, Thermal and Battery
- Hand inference is the most expensive new subsystem. Targets: **≤ 25 ms per frame end-to-end on the reference mid-range device**, and **≤ 15 % additional frame-budget impact on the GL thread** (it shares the GPU if the GPU delegate is used).
- **Adaptive hand tier** (extends Section 5.4): lower analysis resolution → 20 fps → single-hand mode → disable hand tracking and show a banner (gaze/gamepad continue). Uses thermal status and frame-time history.
- **Idle gating**: if no hand has been detected for 10 s, drop to a low-rate "wake-up" probe (e.g., 5 fps), resuming full rate when a hand appears.
- **Passthrough off, hands on**: the camera runs for hand tracking even when the passthrough feed isn't drawn; a persistent camera-active indicator is shown in the HUD in this case.

#### 5.8.6 Privacy & Safety
- Frames for hand tracking are processed **in memory on the device**, never saved or transmitted; only landmark numbers are used downstream.
- Hand control requires the same `CAMERA` permission and an explicit opt-in; a **visible camera-in-use indicator** is always shown while it runs.
- The same stationary-use warning applies whenever the camera is active, and passthrough/hand-control use is never allowed while moving fast (the app can optionally detect high-acceleration motion and show a warning).
- No gesture triggers destructive or purchase-like actions without a confirmation step; a **"Hands off" quick toggle** in the HUD and Control Center disables hand input instantly.

#### 5.8.7 Interaction with ARCore (Mode B)
In ARCore mode, the hand tracker reads ARCore's CPU camera image (`acquireCameraImage`) instead of opening CameraX. Throughput is lower, so the hand tier drops to ~20 fps by default; ARCore planes and anchors keep working. If the device can't deliver both within budget, ARCore keeps priority and hand control falls back to gaze/gamepad with an on-screen note.

